Create a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to write a
Python script with the following specification: Write a single Python executable
script (Termux-style shebang pointing to the Termux Python interpreter, with
`from __future__ import annotations` at the top) whose purpose is to strip type
annotations from Python source files in place, converting annotated code into
valid unannotated Python while preserving formatting, comments, and runtime
behavior as much as possible.
Core requirements: 1.
Purpose and approach: Use the libCST library to parse each target `.py` file
into a concrete syntax tree and apply a custom transformer that removes type
information.
The transformer must: - Remove annotations from function parameters (including
those on parameters of all kinds) by setting the annotation to none, leaving the
default values intact. - Remove return annotations from function definitions. -
Remove PEP 695 type parameter lists from functions and classes when present,
guarded with attribute checks so older libCST versions do not crash. - Convert
annotated assignments: if an annotated assignment has no value, remove the
statement entirely; if it has a value, rewrite it as a plain assignment to the
same target, preserving the value and whitespace/semicolon structure as much as
practical. - Handle any other annotation-only constructs (such as a type alias
statement, if present in the libCST version) appropriately, dropping or
simplifying them so the output remains valid Python. - Leave nodes unchanged
when there is nothing to remove, returning the original updated node. 2.
Inputs and outputs: The script accepts one or more file or directory paths on
the command line (or a sensible default such as the current directory if none is
given).
For directories, it recursively finds all `.py` files, skipping any directory
named `__pycache__`.
Each file is processed and overwritten in place only if the transformed source
differs from the original.
It should report a summary (counts of files processed, changed, skipped, and
failed) to stdout, and per-file errors to stderr. 3.
Parallelism: Process files concurrently using the `multiprocessing` module with
a fixed worker pool of 8 processes.
Use a top-level worker function so it is picklable.
Collect results as they complete and aggregate them in the parent process.
Guard the entry point with the standard main-module check.
Handle the case where there are few files by not spawning more workers than
files. 4.
Robustness and error handling: - Read files with proper encoding detection:
honor PEP 263 encoding declarations via the `tokenize` module's
`detect_encoding`, falling back to UTF-8 on failure, and preserve the original
encoding and line endings when writing back. - Catch libCST parse errors and
other exceptions per file so one bad file does not abort the run; record them as
failures. - Suppress unwanted stdout noise from libraries inside workers using
`contextlib.redirect_stdout` or similar with an `io.StringIO` buffer where
appropriate. - Write files atomically where feasible (write to a temporary file
in the same directory, then replace) to avoid corruption if interrupted. -
Gracefully handle nonexistent paths, unreadable files, and permission errors by
reporting them and continuing. - Use `pathlib.Path` throughout. 5.
Dependencies: Python standard library (`contextlib`, `io`, `multiprocessing`,
`os`, `sys`, `tokenize`, `pathlib`, `typing`) and the third-party `libcst`
package.
Use `TYPE_CHECKING` guards for type-only imports such as `collections.abc`
iterables and sequences. 6.
Execution details: Run as `python script.py [paths...]`.
Exit with status 0 when all files succeed and a nonzero status if any file
failed.
Keep constants such as worker count and skipped directory names as module-level
constants. 7.
Edge cases: empty files, files with no annotations (must not be rewritten),
files with syntax errors, files with BOMs or non-UTF-8 encodings, symlinks (do
not follow directory symlink loops), and deeply nested directories.
Deliver the complete script in a single file, with clear structure, concise
docstrings, and no unnecessary dependencies. ---
